{pad:meta title='Components: slots and declared parameters'/}

<p>A tag of your own with a <code>.pad</code> template is a component: the caller's content goes where the template writes {app:construct 'content'/}. Named slots give it more than one place for content, and <code>{pad:open/}pad:parms{pad:close/}</code> says which parameters it takes. The card below is <code>_tags/card.pad</code>:</p>

{app:source '_tags/card.pad'/}

{common:example 'fragments/components_1'/}

<h2>Slots</h2>

<ul>
  <li>A <code>{pad:open/}pad:slot 'footer'{pad:close/}...{pad:open/}/pad:slot{pad:close/}</code> pair standing directly in the content of the tag is a fill: it is taken out of the content and goes where the template has its <code>{pad:open/}pad:slot 'footer'{pad:close/}</code>. The rest of the content goes to {app:construct 'content'/}.</li>
  <li>In the template, the content of a <code>{pad:open/}pad:slot{pad:close/}</code> is its default, rendered when the caller gave no fill. <code>{pad:open/}pad:slot 'header'/{pad:close/}</code> in the caller's content is an empty fill.</li>
  <li>A default holding {app:construct 'content'/} is a frame: <code>{pad:open/}pad:slot 'footer'{pad:close/}&lt;small&gt;&#64;content&#64;&lt;/small&gt;{pad:open/}/pad:slot{pad:close/}</code> renders the frame round the fill, and nothing - or its {app:construct 'else'/} part - when there is no fill.</li>
  <li>A fill is the caller's text: it renders with the caller's variables, and a <code>{pad:open/}#name{pad:close/}</code> or a <code>{pad:open/}pad:slot{pad:close/}</code> inside it belongs to the caller's tag - which is how a template passes a slot of its own on to a tag it uses.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Every use has its own</h2>

<p>The fills belong to one use of the tag, so a card inside a card keeps its own footer:</p>

{common:example 'fragments/components_2'/}

<h2>Declared parameters</h2>

<ul>
  <li><code>{pad:open/}pad:parms title, tone='plain'/{pad:close/}</code> at the top of the template declares the parameters: a name alone is required, <code>name=default</code> fills in what the caller left out. Both read as <code>{pad:open/}#title{pad:close/}</code>.</li>
  <li>A required parameter left out is an error, and so - under the strict check - is a parameter the tag does not declare: <code>{pad:open/}app:card title='x', titel='y'{pad:close/}</code> is reported by name.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Components written in the template</h2>

<p>A component used on one page only needs no file of its own. <code>{pad:open/}pad:define 'name'{pad:close/}...{pad:open/}/pad:define{pad:close/}</code> writes it in the template: from there on <code>{pad:open/}define:name{pad:close/}</code> renders the body as <code>_tags/name.pad</code> would - <code>{pad:open/}pad:parms{pad:close/}</code>, <code>{pad:open/}#name{pad:close/}</code>, {app:construct 'content'/} and slots work alike.</p>

{common:example 'fragments/components_3'/}

<p>A <code>{pad:open/}pad:macro{pad:close/}</code> is called the way a function is: its parameters are declared on it - a name alone required, <code>name=default</code> filling in - given in their order or by name, and read as fields, <code>{pad:open/}$label{pad:close/}</code>.</p>

{common:example 'fragments/components_4'/}

<ul>
  <li>Both render at every use, in its place and with its fields; both can use themselves, for the rows below the current one.</li>
  <li>A define or macro exists from the point it is walked, as a <code>{pad:open/}pad:content{pad:close/}</code> store does: a use before it is an unknown tag.</li>
  <li>A macro given too many values, missing a required one, or given a name it does not declare is an error.</li>
</ul>
